A||||13||montage||wheel/tire weight||||1||
Z||000000||montage||06-29-2001||11:03 AM||||I'm in the process of looking at new wheels and tires and have some concerns about weight. Has anyone weighed the wheel/tire weight on stock (I have an 88 formula) wheels and tires (or dunlop 245/50/15-225/50/15) and 16 and 17 inch upgrades? The best I found on wheel weight is 15 lbs for rear (how about front 88 6"?) GT stock wheels and most after market wheels appear to be around 20 lbs (it's hard to find weight data). Do the 16 and 17 tires also increase in weight especially compared to 50 series 15"? I know most of the physics with regard to wheel/tire weight but would like some comparisons before I make a decision. I know Racing Hart makes a CP-035 (big bucks) that is around 7 lbs any comments on these?||||reg||1
Z||000001||TRiAD||06-29-2001||11:32 AM||||Any 16 or 17" package not specifically made to be light should be heavier than your stock setup. <BR>There are several wheels out there that are made to be light, like the Racing Hart's you found, but one-piece wheels can be nearly as light and much less expensive than 2 and 3-piece wheels. <P>Kosei K1s are about the least expensive truely light wheels I can think of. The styling is a matter of taste (you either like them or hate them), but for the price, you won't find a lighter wheel. (available in white, silver and anthracite)<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.tirerack.com/wheels/images/kosei/ko_k1_racing_w_a.jpg">  <P>SSR usually makes light wheels as well, and the "Competition" wheel looks like it would fit the bill too, but I haven't been able to find weight info on it. (anthracite only)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.tirerack.com/wheels/images/ssr/ssr_comp_a.jpg"> <P>You'll want to make sure your aspect ratio (circumferance of wheel/tire combo) remains as close to stock as possible, or you will lose useable torque, and you are right for looking for lighter wheel/tire combos, because increased unsprung weight will cost you performance, as well. One thing many people don't realize is not only is accelleration inhibited by heavy rolling stock, but braking is affected even more. <P>Good luck!<P><BR>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg">  <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I><p>[This message has been edited by TRiAD (edited 06-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||AkursedX||06-29-2001||12:08 PM||||When I bought my 17 inch tire/wheel setup, I weighed both the stock and the new setup.  I can't remember the exact weights of everything, but as a whole, my 17inch setup weighs 30lbs more than the stock setup.  <P>It's an acceptable comprimise for omproved looks and handling.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||06-29-2001||03:59 PM||||Don't forget that 30lbs extra unsprung weight has a heigher impact on performance. And centripetal force on the wheels makes the weight act heavier at speed.  Forgive me if I'm a little vague here - been a long time since Engineering school.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Basically, since the wheels are spinning, the impact is that of much more than 30lbs.  Anyone else with fresher memory care to elaborate?||||reg||3
Z||000004||AkursedX||06-29-2001||04:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR>[B]Don't forget that 30lbs extra unsprung weight has a heigher impact on performance. And centripetal force on the wheels makes the weight act heavier at speed.  Forgive me if I'm a little vague here - been a long time since Engineering school.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, I found the numbers where I had the differences, and the difference was actually only 22lbs.  5lb difference for each front wheel, and 6lb, for each rear wheel.  <P>And yes, it does make the wheel heavier at higher speeds, but it still has definietly had a positive effect on my handling.  The lower sidewalls, and wider tires provide for much better handling over the stock setup.  <P>But if you want lighter rim, I would go with the SSR ones mentioned previously.  They are probably the lightest that you will find, but you will be spending a good buck on them.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||TRiAD||06-29-2001||04:53 PM||||17"s are $375/ea @ tire rack. <BR>($1540 shipped + tires)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||TRiAD||06-29-2001||04:53 PM||||oops<BR><p>[This message has been edited by TRiAD (edited 06-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||montage||06-29-2001||04:55 PM||||I'm finding that most after market wheels, except a few, are up to 10 lbs heavier than stock. So my question now is would handling be better with stock wheels and 245-50-15 rear 225-50-15 front Dunlop 8000 than going to 17" wheels and 40 series 245 on rear and 225 on the front? Most racers do not use low profile tires due to the two basic facts, you lose road feel with a low profile (break lose without warning) and the contact patch with the road is larger with a high profile tire due to sidewall flex. I looked up some racing tires and they are usually 45 to 50 series and higher. I don't want to spend the money if the handling isn't improved. I really don't care about looks only performance. Any commnets?||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||06-29-2001||05:19 PM||||I did and surprisingly the 6” wheels weighed about the same as my 7” wheels. They were 18 lbs.  Go here <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/010204.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/010204.html</A>  and see. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Dave Gunsul||06-29-2001||09:44 PM||||I have the silver Kosei's shown above on my GT. The stock GT rims usually weigh from 18-22 IB's. The 15 Kosei's weigh 13-13.5 IB's.<P>Less weight will also help handling so if<BR>you want the best of both worlds go for a set<BR>of 16inch SSR's with 50 series rubber.<BR>That way you'll benefit from the short sidewall and the decreased weight!<P>The SSR's weight is as follows:<BR>15x6= 8.6-8.9 IB's<BR>15x7= 9.6-10.1 IB's<BR>16x7= 11.3-11.9 IB's<BR>17x7.5= 13.7-14.1<BR>I would-a got a set of these myself but they weren't out when I bought my Koseis.<BR>I did notice a difference with the Kosei's over my stock GT rims in handling and acceleration! I've got the 15x7's BTW.<P>Kosei's weight as follows:<BR>15x7= 12.8-13.5 IBs<BR>15x8= 13.8-14.5<BR>16x7.5= 15.0-15.5<BR>17x7.5= 16.0-16.5<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000010||montage||06-29-2001||10:35 PM||||Thanks for all the information. Since the stock wheel is about 18 lbs I may now look for a lighter wheel. Some wheels I looked at are Konig and their site has weight listed <A HREF="http://www.konigwheels.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.konigwheels.com/</A>  and run from 17  -23 lbs for the sizes and type I looked at. <P>Tire rack gave these specs to me:<BR>17X7 SSR 17.5 (can't remember the model)<BR>same as Dave for the Kosei K1 but they only listed the 15X7 and 17X7 didn't know they had a 15X8.<BR>Most others were 21 to 25 lbs.<BR> <BR>I also looked at  TSW Revo due to available offsets<BR>16X7 47 mm 20-25 lbs<BR>17X8 35 mm 27 lbs<BR>I was set to get these but the weight has changed my mind. <P>So are the Racing Harts CP-035 the lightest? Told they are ~ 7lbs for 17X8. Now if I could just talk the wife into it. Then the next question is 16" with 45 or 50 series or 17" with 40 or 45 series or is actual side wall height (since it is based on the width and aspect ratio) more important to consider?||||reg||1
Z||000011||TRiAD||06-29-2001||10:49 PM||||Sounds like you've got it about sorted out. <BR>Glad you asked, TSWs <B>S U C K !</B><BR>The offsets are never right, and they're HEAVY!<P>Good luck, let us know how it goes!||||reg||1
Z||000012||Dave Gunsul||06-30-2001||12:00 PM||||Here are some other light weight wheels to consider.<P>BBS RK weight: 15x7 = 14.2 IB's<BR>               16x7 = 15.5<BR>               16x8 = 15.1-16.1<BR>               17x7 = 17.2<P>SSR GTI weight: 16x7 = 15.0-15.5<BR>                17x7 = 16.5<BR>                17x8 = 17.5-18.0<P>SSR Integral weight: 16x7 = 15.5<BR>                     16x8 = 16.0-17.0<BR>                     17x7 = 16.0-17.5<P>Some of these wheels come in 18's and I have the weight for them too. If anyones interested i'll post them.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000013||Dave Gunsul||07-01-2001||11:29 AM||||Bump.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||10
